Logic is the foundation upon which all of programming language theory is built. Every formal semantics, every type system, every program verification rests on logical foundations. The propositions we prove about programs, the types we assign to expressions, the invariants we establish about loop iterations---all are logical assertions, and their validity is established by logical reasoning.

This chapter develops the logical foundations needed throughout the rest of the book. We begin with propositional logic---the logic of truth values and Boolean connectives---and then move to first-order logic, which adds quantification over individuals. Along the way, we develop proof systems (natural deduction, sequent calculus, resolution) and establish fundamental metatheoretic results (soundness, completeness, decidability).

\section{Propositional Logic: Syntax}\label{sec:logic-prop-syntax}

\subsection{The Language of Propositional Logic}

Propositional logic is the simplest non-trivial logic. Its language consists of \emph{propositional variables} combined using \emph{logical connectives} to form \emph{formulas}.

\begin{definition}[Propositional Formulas]\label{def:prop-formulas}
The set of \emph{propositional formulas} (or \emph{well-formed formulas}, wffs) is defined inductively by the following grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Propositional Formulas}
\varphi & \longrightarrow p \mid \top \mid \bot \mid \neg\varphi \mid \varphi \land \varphi \mid \varphi \lor \varphi \mid \varphi \to \varphi \mid \varphi \leftrightarrow \varphi
\end{grammar}
where $p$ ranges over a countably infinite set of \emph{propositional variables} $\{p_0, p_1, p_2, \ldots\}$.
\end{definition}

The connectives have the following intended meanings:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\top$ (truth): always true
  \item $\bot$ (falsity): always false
  \item $\neg\varphi$ (negation): ``not $\varphi$''
  \item $\varphi \land \psi$ (conjunction): ``$\varphi$ and $\psi$''
  \item $\varphi \lor \psi$ (disjunction): ``$\varphi$ or $\psi$''
  \item $\varphi \to \psi$ (implication): ``if $\varphi$ then $\psi$''
  \item $\varphi \leftrightarrow \psi$ (biconditional): ``$\varphi$ if and only if $\psi$''
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Subformulas]\label{def:subformulas}
The set $\mathrm{sub}(\varphi)$ of \emph{subformulas} of a formula $\varphi$ is defined recursively:
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{sub}(p) &= \{p\} \\
  \mathrm{sub}(\top) &= \{\top\} \\
  \mathrm{sub}(\bot) &= \{\bot\} \\
  \mathrm{sub}(\neg\varphi) &= \{\neg\varphi\} \cup \mathrm{sub}(\varphi) \\
  \mathrm{sub}(\varphi \circ \psi) &= \{\varphi \circ \psi\} \cup \mathrm{sub}(\varphi) \cup \mathrm{sub}(\psi)
\end{align*}
where $\circ$ is any binary connective.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Formula Size]\label{def:formula-size}
The \emph{size} $|\varphi|$ of a formula $\varphi$ is the number of connectives and variables occurring in $\varphi$, defined recursively:
\begin{align*}
  |p| &= 1, \quad |\top| = 1, \quad |\bot| = 1 \\
  |\neg\varphi| &= 1 + |\varphi| \\
  |\varphi \circ \psi| &= 1 + |\varphi| + |\psi|
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Notational Conventions}

We adopt the following precedence conventions (from highest to lowest):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\neg$ (highest precedence)
  \item $\land$
  \item $\lor$
  \item $\to$
  \item $\leftrightarrow$ (lowest precedence)
\end{enumerate}

All connectives associate to the right, so $\varphi \to \psi \to \chi$ means $\varphi \to (\psi \to \chi)$. Parentheses override these conventions.

\section{Propositional Logic: Semantics}\label{sec:logic-prop-semantics}

\subsection{Truth Assignments and Satisfaction}

The semantics of propositional logic is given by \emph{truth assignments}---functions that assign truth values to propositional variables.

\begin{definition}[Truth Assignment]\label{def:truth-assignment}
A \emph{truth assignment} (or \emph{valuation}) is a function $v : \mathrm{Var} \to \{\mathbf{T}, \mathbf{F}\}$, where $\mathrm{Var}$ is the set of propositional variables.
\end{definition}

Every truth assignment extends uniquely to all formulas:

\begin{definition}[Truth Value of a Formula]\label{def:truth-value}
Given a truth assignment $v$, the \emph{truth value} $\hat{v}(\varphi)$ of a formula $\varphi$ is defined recursively:
\begin{align*}
  \hat{v}(p) &= v(p) \\
  \hat{v}(\top) &= \mathbf{T} \\
  \hat{v}(\bot) &= \mathbf{F} \\
  \hat{v}(\neg\varphi) &= \begin{cases} \mathbf{T} & \text{if } \hat{v}(\varphi) = \mathbf{F} \\ \mathbf{F} & \text{if } \hat{v}(\varphi) = \mathbf{T} \end{cases} \\
  \hat{v}(\varphi \land \psi) &= \begin{cases} \mathbf{T} & \text{if } \hat{v}(\varphi) = \mathbf{T} \text{ and } \hat{v}(\psi) = \mathbf{T} \\ \mathbf{F} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \\
  \hat{v}(\varphi \lor \psi) &= \begin{cases} \mathbf{F} & \text{if } \hat{v}(\varphi) = \mathbf{F} \text{ and } \hat{v}(\psi) = \mathbf{F} \\ \mathbf{T} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \\
  \hat{v}(\varphi \to \psi) &= \begin{cases} \mathbf{F} & \text{if } \hat{v}(\varphi) = \mathbf{T} \text{ and } \hat{v}(\psi) = \mathbf{F} \\ \mathbf{T} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Models and Validity]\label{def:models-validity}
Let $\varphi$ be a formula and $v$ a truth assignment.
\begin{itemize}
  \item We write $v \models \varphi$ (``$v$ satisfies $\varphi$'' or ``$v$ is a \emph{model} of $\varphi$'') if $\hat{v}(\varphi) = \mathbf{T}$.
  \item $\varphi$ is \emph{satisfiable} if there exists $v$ such that $v \models \varphi$.
  \item $\varphi$ is \emph{valid} (or a \emph{tautology}), written $\models \varphi$, if for all $v$, $v \models \varphi$.
  \item $\varphi$ is \emph{unsatisfiable} (or a \emph{contradiction}) if no $v$ satisfies $\varphi$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Semantic Consequence]\label{def:semantic-consequence}
A formula $\varphi$ is a \emph{semantic consequence} of a set of formulas $\Gamma$, written $\Gamma \models \varphi$, if every truth assignment that satisfies all formulas in $\Gamma$ also satisfies $\varphi$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Truth Tables}

Truth tables provide a mechanical method for determining validity and satisfiability. For a formula with $n$ distinct propositional variables, the truth table has $2^n$ rows.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Truth table for $(p \to q) \lor (q \to p)$}
\label{tab:truth-table-example}
\begin{tabular}{cc|c|c|c}
$p$ & $q$ & $p \to q$ & $q \to p$ & $(p \to q) \lor (q \to p)$ \\
\hline
$\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ \\
$\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{F}$ & $\mathbf{F}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ \\
$\mathbf{F}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{F}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ \\
$\mathbf{F}$ & $\mathbf{F}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ & $\mathbf{T}$ \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Since every row yields $\mathbf{T}$, the formula is valid. This is an instance of a general fact: for any two propositions, at least one implies the other.

\begin{theorem}[Validity via Truth Tables]\label{thm:truth-table-validity}
A propositional formula $\varphi$ is valid if and only if every row of its truth table assigns $\mathbf{T}$ to $\varphi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By structural induction on $\varphi$, using the recursive definition of $\hat{v}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Propositional Logic: Proof Systems}\label{sec:logic-prop-proofs}

A proof system provides a syntactic method for establishing validity. The key property we require is that the proof system is \emph{sound} (only valid formulas are provable) and \emph{complete} (all valid formulas are provable).

\subsection{Natural Deduction}

Natural deduction, introduced by Gentzen in 1935, is a proof system that closely mirrors natural mathematical reasoning. It consists of \emph{introduction} and \emph{elimination} rules for each connective.

\begin{definition}[Natural Deduction for Propositional Logic]\label{def:nat-ded-prop}
A \emph{natural deduction proof} is a tree of formulas, where each node is justified by one of the following rules. We write $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ to mean ``there is a natural deduction proof of $\varphi$ from assumptions $\Gamma$.''

\emph{Assumption}: $\varphi \in \Gamma$ implies $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$.

\emph{Conjunction}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \quad \Gamma \vdash \psi}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \land \psi}\textsc{($\land$I)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \land \psi}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi}\textsc{($\land$E$_1$)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \land \psi}{\Gamma \vdash \psi}\textsc{($\land$E$_2$)}$
\end{center}

\emph{Disjunction}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \varphi}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \lor \psi}\textsc{($\lor$I$_1$)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \psi}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \lor \psi}\textsc{($\lor$I$_2$)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \lor \psi \quad \Gamma, \varphi \vdash \chi \quad \Gamma, \psi \vdash \chi}{\Gamma \vdash \chi}\textsc{($\lor$E)}$
\end{center}

\emph{Implication}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma, \varphi \vdash \psi}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \to \psi}\textsc{($\to$I)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \varphi \to \psi \quad \Gamma \vdash \varphi}{\Gamma \vdash \psi}\textsc{($\to$E)}$
\end{center}

\emph{Negation}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma, \varphi \vdash \bot}{\Gamma \vdash \neg\varphi}\textsc{($\neg$I)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \neg\neg\varphi}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi}\textsc{(DNE)}$
\end{center}

\emph{Bottom}:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \bot}{\Gamma \vdash \varphi}\textsc{($\bot$E)}$
\end{center}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[A Natural Deduction Proof]\label{ex:nat-ded-proof}
We prove $\vdash p \to (q \to p)$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $p$ --- assumption (label 1)
  \item $q$ --- assumption (label 2)
  \item $p$ --- by assumption 1
  \item $q \to p$ --- by $\to$I, discharging assumption 2
  \item $p \to (q \to p)$ --- by $\to$I, discharging assumption 1
\end{enumerate}
\end{example}

\subsection{Sequent Calculus}

Gentzen's sequent calculus provides an alternative proof system that is particularly well-suited to proof-theoretic analysis.

\begin{definition}[Sequent]\label{def:sequent}
A \emph{sequent} is an expression $\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta$, where $\Gamma$ and $\Delta$ are finite (possibly empty) sets of formulas. The sequent $\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta$ is read as ``if all formulas in $\Gamma$ are true, then at least one formula in $\Delta$ is true.''
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Sequent Calculus $\mathbf{LK}$]\label{def:sequent-calc}
The sequent calculus $\mathbf{LK}$ for classical propositional logic consists of the following rules:

\emph{Axiom}: $\varphi \Rightarrow \varphi$

\emph{Structural rules}:
\begin{center}
Weakening: $\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta}{\Gamma, \varphi \Rightarrow \Delta}$ \quad $\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta}{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \varphi}$

Contraction: $\dfrac{\Gamma, \varphi, \varphi \Rightarrow \Delta}{\Gamma, \varphi \Rightarrow \Delta}$ \quad $\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \varphi, \varphi}{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \varphi}$

Cut: $\dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \varphi \quad \varphi, \Sigma \Rightarrow \Pi}{\Gamma, \Sigma \Rightarrow \Delta, \Pi}$
\end{center}

\emph{Logical rules} (left and right introduction for each connective):
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\Gamma, \varphi \Rightarrow \Delta}{\Gamma, \varphi \land \psi \Rightarrow \Delta}\textsc{($\land$L$_1$)} \qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \varphi \quad \Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \psi}{\Gamma \Rightarrow \Delta, \varphi \land \psi}\textsc{($\land$R)}$
\end{center}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cut Elimination (Gentzen's Hauptsatz)]\label{thm:cut-elimination}
Every sequent provable in $\mathbf{LK}$ has a cut-free proof.
\end{theorem}

Cut elimination is one of the most important results in proof theory. It implies that proofs can be normalized to a form where every formula in the proof is a subformula of the conclusion---the \emph{subformula property}. This has profound implications for program verification, as we discuss in \chref{proof-theory}.

\subsection{Resolution}

Resolution is a proof system designed for automated theorem proving. It operates on formulas in \emph{conjunctive normal form} (CNF).

\begin{definition}[Conjunctive Normal Form]\label{def:cnf}
A formula is in \emph{conjunctive normal form} if it is a conjunction of \emph{clauses}, where each clause is a disjunction of \emph{literals} (a literal is a propositional variable or its negation).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Resolution Rule]\label{def:resolution}
The \emph{resolution rule} states:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{C \lor p \quad D \lor \neg p}{C \lor D}\textsc{(Res)}$
\end{center}
where $p$ is a propositional variable, and $C, D$ are clauses.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness and Completeness of Resolution]\label{thm:resolution}
A set of clauses $S$ is unsatisfiable if and only if the empty clause $\square$ can be derived from $S$ by repeated application of the resolution rule.
\end{theorem}

Resolution is the basis of modern SAT solvers, which are used extensively in program verification, hardware verification, and automated reasoning.

\section{First-Order Logic: Syntax}\label{sec:logic-fol-syntax}

Propositional logic cannot express statements about individuals and their properties. First-order logic extends propositional logic with \emph{terms} (denoting individuals), \emph{predicates} (denoting properties and relations), and \emph{quantifiers} ($\forall$, $\exists$).

\subsection{Signatures and Terms}

\begin{definition}[Signature]\label{def:signature}
A \emph{signature} (or \emph{vocabulary}) $\Sigma$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A set $\mathcal{F}$ of \emph{function symbols}, each with an associated arity $n \geq 0$. Function symbols of arity 0 are called \emph{constants}.
  \item A set $\mathcal{P}$ of \emph{predicate symbols}, each with an associated arity $n \geq 0$. Predicate symbols of arity 0 correspond to propositional variables.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Terms]\label{def:terms}
Given a signature $\Sigma$ and a countably infinite set $\mathrm{Var}$ of \emph{variables}, the set of \emph{terms} is defined inductively:
\begin{grammar}{Terms}
t & \longrightarrow x \mid c \mid f(t_1, \ldots, t_n)
\end{grammar}
where $x \in \mathrm{Var}$, $c$ is a constant symbol, and $f$ is an $n$-ary function symbol.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Terms in Arithmetic]\label{ex:terms-arithmetic}
For the signature of arithmetic $\Sigma = \{0, S, +, \times\}$ where $0$ is a constant, $S$ is unary (successor), and $+, \times$ are binary, some terms are:
\[0, \quad S(0), \quad S(S(0)), \quad x + S(0), \quad x \times (y + S(S(0)))\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Formulas of First-Order Logic}

\begin{definition}[First-Order Formulas]\label{def:fol-formulas}
Given a signature $\Sigma$, the set of \emph{first-order formulas} is defined inductively:
\begin{grammar}{First-Order Formulas}
\varphi & \longrightarrow P(t_1, \ldots, t_n) \mid \top \mid \bot \mid \neg\varphi \mid \varphi \land \varphi \mid \varphi \lor \varphi \mid \varphi \to \varphi \mid \forall x.\,\varphi \mid \exists x.\,\varphi
\end{grammar}
where $P$ is an $n$-ary predicate symbol and $t_1, \ldots, t_n$ are terms.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Free and Bound Variables]\label{def:free-bound}
The set of \emph{free variables} $\mathrm{fv}(\varphi)$ of a formula $\varphi$ is defined recursively:
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{fv}(P(t_1, \ldots, t_n)) &= \bigcup_i \mathrm{fv}(t_i) \\
  \mathrm{fv}(\neg\varphi) &= \mathrm{fv}(\varphi) \\
  \mathrm{fv}(\varphi \circ \psi) &= \mathrm{fv}(\varphi) \cup \mathrm{fv}(\psi) \\
  \mathrm{fv}(\forall x.\,\varphi) &= \mathrm{fv}(\varphi) \setminus \{x\} \\
  \mathrm{fv}(\exists x.\,\varphi) &= \mathrm{fv}(\varphi) \setminus \{x\}
\end{align*}
A variable is \emph{bound} if it is not free. A formula with no free variables is called a \emph{sentence}.
\end{definition}

\section{First-Order Logic: Semantics}\label{sec:logic-fol-semantics}

\subsection{Structures and Interpretations}

\begin{definition}[Structure]\label{def:structure}
A \emph{structure} (or \emph{model}, or \emph{interpretation}) $\mathcal{M}$ for a signature $\Sigma$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A non-empty set $M$ called the \emph{domain} (or \emph{universe})
  \item For each $n$-ary function symbol $f \in \mathcal{F}$, a function $f^{\mathcal{M}} : M^n \to M$
  \item For each $n$-ary predicate symbol $P \in \mathcal{P}$, a relation $P^{\mathcal{M}} \subseteq M^n$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Variable Assignment]\label{def:var-assignment}
A \emph{variable assignment} in a structure $\mathcal{M}$ is a function $\rho : \mathrm{Var} \to M$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Interpretation of Terms]\label{def:term-interp}
Given a structure $\mathcal{M}$ and assignment $\rho$, the \emph{value} $\sembrack{t}_{\mathcal{M},\rho}$ of a term $t$ is:
\begin{align*}
  \sembrack{x}_{\mathcal{M},\rho} &= \rho(x) \\
  \sembrack{c}_{\mathcal{M},\rho} &= c^{\mathcal{M}} \\
  \sembrack{f(t_1, \ldots, t_n)}_{\mathcal{M},\rho} &= f^{\mathcal{M}}(\sembrack{t_1}_{\mathcal{M},\rho}, \ldots, \sembrack{t_n}_{\mathcal{M},\rho})
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Satisfaction]\label{def:satisfaction}
The \emph{satisfaction relation} $\mathcal{M}, \rho \models \varphi$ is defined recursively:
\begin{align*}
  \mathcal{M}, \rho &\models P(t_1, \ldots, t_n) &&\iff (\sembrack{t_1}_{\mathcal{M},\rho}, \ldots, \sembrack{t_n}_{\mathcal{M},\rho}) \in P^{\mathcal{M}} \\
  \mathcal{M}, \rho &\models \neg\varphi &&\iff \mathcal{M}, \rho \not\models \varphi \\
  \mathcal{M}, \rho &\models \varphi \land \psi &&\iff \mathcal{M}, \rho \models \varphi \text{ and } \mathcal{M}, \rho \models \psi \\
  \mathcal{M}, \rho &\models \forall x.\,\varphi &&\iff \text{for all } d \in M, \mathcal{M}, \rho[x \mapsto d] \models \varphi \\
  \mathcal{M}, \rho &\models \exists x.\,\varphi &&\iff \text{there exists } d \in M \text{ such that } \mathcal{M}, \rho[x \mapsto d] \models \varphi
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\section{Soundness and Completeness}\label{sec:logic-soundness-completeness}

The central metatheoretic results for first-order logic connect the syntactic notion of provability with the semantic notion of validity.

\begin{definition}[Provability]\label{def:provability}
We write $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ to mean that $\varphi$ is provable from assumptions $\Gamma$ in some fixed proof system (e.g., natural deduction or sequent calculus).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness]\label{thm:soundness}
If $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$, then $\Gamma \models \varphi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the structure of the proof. Each inference rule preserves truth: if the premises are true in all models, so is the conclusion. The detailed proof proceeds by checking each rule of the proof system.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness (G\"odel, 1930)]\label{thm:completeness}
If $\Gamma \models \varphi$, then $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$.
\end{theorem}

This is G\"odel's celebrated completeness theorem. Its proof, via the construction of a \emph{Henkin model}, is one of the landmarks of 20th-century logic.

\begin{corollary}[Consistency and Satisfiability]\label{cor:consistency-sat}
A set of first-order sentences is consistent (has no contradiction derivable from it) if and only if it is satisfiable (has a model).
\end{corollary}

\section{Decidability and Undecidability}\label{sec:logic-decidability}

\begin{theorem}[Decidability of Propositional Logic]\label{thm:prop-decidable}
Propositional logic is decidable: there exists an algorithm that, given a formula $\varphi$, determines whether $\models \varphi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Truth tables provide a decision procedure. However, the truth table method requires $2^n$ rows for $n$ variables, which is exponential. The question of whether propositional validity can be decided in polynomial time is the famous $\mathbf{P}$ vs.\ $\mathbf{NP}$ problem (see \chref{computability}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Undecidability of First-Order Logic (Church--Turing, 1936)]\label{thm:fol-undecidable}
First-order logic is undecidable: there is no algorithm that, given an arbitrary first-order sentence $\varphi$, determines whether $\models \varphi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By reduction from the halting problem. Given a Turing machine $M$ and input $w$, one can construct a first-order sentence $\varphi_{M,w}$ such that $\models \varphi_{M,w}$ if and only if $M$ halts on $w$. Since the halting problem is undecidable, so is first-order validity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semi-Decidability of First-Order Logic]\label{thm:fol-semidecidable}
First-order validity is \emph{semi-decidable}: there exists an algorithm that, given a valid sentence $\varphi$, eventually confirms its validity (but may not terminate on invalid sentences).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Enumerate all possible proofs and check each one. By completeness, every valid sentence has a proof, so this procedure will eventually find it.
\end{proof}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Decidability landscape for logics}
\label{tab:decidability}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Logic} & \textbf{Decidable?} & \textbf{Semi-decidable?} \\
\hline
Propositional & Yes & Yes \\
First-order & No & Yes \\
Monadic first-order & Yes & Yes \\
Two-variable fragment & Yes & Yes \\
Second-order & No & No \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Applications to Program Verification}\label{sec:logic-verification}

Logic is not merely a theoretical curiosity; it is the foundation of modern program verification.

\subsection{Hoare Logic}

Hoare logic uses first-order logic to reason about imperative programs. A \emph{Hoare triple} $\{P\}\;c\;\{Q\}$ asserts that if precondition $P$ holds before command $c$ executes, then postcondition $Q$ holds afterward.

\begin{semrules}{Hoare Logic}
\textsc{Assign} \quad \overline{\{Q[e/x]\}\; x := e \;\{Q\}} \\[6pt]
\textsc{Seq} \quad \dfrac{\{P\}\;c_1\;\{R\} \quad \{R\}\;c_2\;\{Q\}}{\{P\}\; c_1; c_2 \;\{Q\}} \\[6pt]
\textsc{If} \quad \dfrac{\{P \land b\}\;c_1\;\{Q\} \quad \{P \land \neg b\}\;c_2\;\{Q\}}{\{P\}\; \textbf{if}\;b\;\textbf{then}\;c_1\;\textbf{else}\;c_2\;\{Q\}} \\[6pt]
\textsc{While} \quad \dfrac{\{I \land b\}\;c\;\{I\}}{\{I\}\; \textbf{while}\;b\;c\;\{I \land \neg b\}}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Verification Conditions}

Given a program annotated with preconditions, postconditions, and loop invariants, a \emph{verification condition generator} produces a set of first-order formulas whose validity implies the correctness of the program. These formulas are then discharged by automated theorem provers or SMT solvers.

\begin{insight}
The connection between logic and program verification is not coincidental. The Curry--Howard correspondence (see \chref{proof-theory}) reveals that programs and proofs are fundamentally the same kind of object. A verified program is one whose type constitutes a proof of its specification. This insight, which emerged from the study of logic in the 20th century, is becoming a practical reality in the 21st.
\end{insight}

\subsection{SMT Solvers}

Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) solvers combine propositional SAT solving with decision procedures for specific theories (arithmetic, arrays, bit vectors). They are the workhorses of modern program verification, used in tools like Z3, CVC4, and Dafny.

\begin{example}[SMT Verification]\label{ex:smt-verification}
Consider the program:
\begin{verbatim}
  // Precondition: x >= 0
  y = x + 1;
  // Postcondition: y > 0
\end{verbatim}
The verification condition is: $x \geq 0 \implies x + 1 > 0$. An SMT solver can discharge this automatically using the theory of linear arithmetic.
\end{example}

\section{Exercises}\label{sec:logic-exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Prove that $(p \to q) \to ((q \to r) \to (p \to r))$ is valid using (a) truth tables, (b) natural deduction.
  
  \item Convert the formula $(p \lor q) \to r$ to conjunctive normal form.
  
  \item Prove that the resolution rule is sound: if $C \lor p$ and $D \lor \neg p$ are both true, then $C \lor D$ is true.
  
  \item Write a first-order sentence expressing ``every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes'' (Goldbach's conjecture).
  
  \item Show that the set $\{\forall x.\,P(x), \exists x.\,\neg P(x)\}$ is unsatisfiable.
  
  \item Prove that first-order logic with only unary predicates (monadic first-order logic) is decidable.
  
  \item Give a natural deduction proof of $\vdash \neg(p \land \neg p)$ (the law of non-contradiction).
  
  \item Show that $\forall x.\,(P(x) \to Q(x)), \forall x.\,P(x) \models \forall x.\,Q(x)$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Chapter Summary}\label{sec:logic-summary}

We have developed the foundations of propositional and first-order logic: their syntax, semantics, and proof systems. The key results are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Propositional logic is decidable but NP-complete.
  \item First-order logic is undecidable but semi-decidable (G\"odel's completeness theorem).
  \item Natural deduction, sequent calculus, and resolution provide sound and complete proof systems.
  \item Cut elimination gives proofs the subformula property.
  \item Logic is the foundation of program verification via Hoare logic and SMT solving.
\end{itemize}

These logical foundations will be used throughout the rest of the book. In the next chapter, we turn to set theory and relations---the mathematical structures that provide the language for talking about collections, functions, and orderings.
